course: dictionary
lesson: 13096
title: Words of warmth and home
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fireside is a word made of two smaller words, fire and side. The fireside is the area right next to a fireplace, where people sit to keep warm.
explain: Read this: «After the snowy walk, Mum put the kettle on and we sat by the fireside with hot chocolate.» The cold, the hot drink and sitting close together tell you the fireside is a warm, cosy place.
explain: Fireside can also mean home and family life, a feeling of being safe. «Mole was glad to be back at the fireside.» Mole is not just warm; he feels at home.
explain: Fireside can also describe things. In «a fireside story», the word tells you the story is told at home, by the fire. Look at the words next to it to find its job.
fact: Fireside is a noun: the fireside. It can also be an adjective: a fireside chat.
fact: «Fireside» means the area right next to a fireplace, where people sit to keep warm.
fact: Figuratively, «fireside» means home and family life, cosy and safe.
fact: As an adjective, «fireside» means happening by the fire at home, as in «fireside stories».
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Mole and Rat sit by the fireside in Badger's kitchen, and the fireside stands for home.
exercise: «The old dog slept snugly by the fireside all evening.» Where did the dog sleep? | In the garden in the rain | On the cold school bus | Under the bed in the dark | *By the fireside, right next to the fireplace
exercise: «We loved hearing fireside stories before bed.» What does fireside mean in this sentence? | Stories written in a bookshop | Stories about fire engines | *Stories told at home by the fire | Stories that are very scary
exercise: Mum says, «Come home to the fireside, love, it's freezing out here.» What does fireside stand for? | *Home, warmth and feeling safe | A cold walk in the snow | A fire engine | A park bench
test: «Grandad read us stories at the fireside while the rain tapped on the window.» What does fireside mean here? | A busy road | Outside in the cold | *A cosy, safe time at home by the fire | A garden shed
test: Which describes a fireside chat best? | A letter sent by post | A loud shout across a football pitch | A chat about maths homework at school | *A friendly talk held at home by the fire
exam: Mole felt at home again when he sat by the fireside, so fireside here most likely means? | A dangerous forest | A school hall | A cold, empty road | *Home and comfort
